<h2 class="mt-12">Product care matchup FAQ</h2>
<section class="max-w-3xl mx-auto space-y-6">
<div><h3>Klaviyo or Shopify Email for product care?</h3><p>Product Care beginners do well on native email; Klaviyo fits when product care logic leans on events, catalog data, and segment reporting, with profile billing modeled on product care expansion.</p></div>
<div><h3>Omnisend vs Klaviyo for product care?</h3><p>Omnisend gets product care teams sending sooner with light SMS attached; Klaviyo stretches product care segmentation further. Test-drive one real product care flow each way before judging.</p></div>
<div><h3>Mailchimp or Klaviyo for product care?</h3><p>For product care, Mailchimp trades on familiarity, Klaviyo on Shopify event fidelity; settle product care pricing by checking both official pages at your contacts.</p></div>
<div><h3>Do I need a separate SMS tool for product care?</h3><p>For product care, skip dedicated SMS initially — bundled texting suffices until SMS provably lifts product care results. Confirm product care consent, quiet hours, and per-message pricing, and prove the product care audience answers texts.</p></div>
<div><h3>How should I suppress audiences in product care flows?</h3><p>Product Care suppression: fresh purchasers, active cases, refunded orders, and consent gaps all exit. Document product care exits per journey; product care suppression errors dwarf missed-campaign cost.</p></div>
<div><h3>What does product care email cost?</h3><p>Costs combine the platform subscription, contact or send overages, SMS credits, template and creative work, and the discount budget your product care campaigns consume. Plans and limits shift often, so check the official pricing pages for your product care shortlist and model an eighteen-month total before committing.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which app should a lean team pilot first for product care?</h3><p>Open product care with the tool at full command in fourteen days — simple product care sends natively, branched product care on a lean platform. Shipping product care beats architecting it.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I measure product care email results?</h3><p>Measure product care on contribution, not vanity sends: margin per send, repeat purchase, unsubscribes, complaints, and support load alongside attributed revenue, with a holdout cohort proving the product care journeys caused the lift.</p></div>
<div><h3>Can I run product care email without an agency?</h3><p>Yes, if the scope stays small. Pick one product care journey, document consent and suppression rules, and reuse a simple template system. For product care, hire help only if journey complexity, inbox-placement remediation, or peak product care volume genuinely exceeds what the team can run.</p></div>
<div><h3>When should I graduate from my first app for product care?</h3><p>The product care moment to graduate arrives when safe editing, purchase-state segmentation, or cost forecasting at your product care contact scale stops working. For product care, that moment usually arrives when more than two people maintain flows or when peak campaigns require documented suppression.</p></div>
<div><h3>How much discounting is acceptable for product care?</h3><p>Treat discounts as one lever, not the default. For product care, test content-led recovery and loyalty first, cap discount depth against margin, and document who can approve exceptions. A product care program where nearly every order needs a discount is signalling weak product care value, not wrong pricing.</p></div>
<div><h3>Which Shopify data matters most for product care?</h3><p>Order and refund state, cart and browse events, consent source, and product availability cover most product care decisions. On product care, prove events in a test checkout before trusting them in flows, with product care field definitions written where both teams can find them.</p></div>
<div><h3>How do I avoid duplicate sends across apps for product care?</h3><p>Give one platform ownership of each product care journey, document which app sends what, and share suppression lists where the tools support it. Keep a weekly product care peak-season audit: pull sample customers, list each product care message they got, flag overlaps.</p></div>
<div><h3>What should a product care pilot include?</h3><p>For product care, a tight pilot wins: single audience, at most two journeys, explicit suppression, a holdout, and a day-thirty product care review of margin and complaints with pre-agreed pass/fail lines.</p></div>
</section>
